description:
FILED s543m (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27), from the reviewing session's review of s543m (F9).  PRE-EXISTING: the base 9a3ce86f prints `(?^:x)`; s543m's #5402 added the `u` (as perl does) and left the `n` out, as it was.

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 | ./runpcl; scratch/s543m/p11/rows.pl prog 8):
    use v5.22; print qr/x/n, " ", qr/x/in, " ", qr/(a)/n, "\n";
    no feature "unicode_strings"; print qr/x/n, "\n";
perl: (?^un:x) (?^uin:x) (?^un:(a)) / (?^n:x).  PCL tree: (?^u:x) (?^ui:x) (?^u:(a)) / (?^:x).  Base: (?^:x) (?^i:x) (?^:(a)) / (?^:x).

WHERE: the qr's stringification builds its flag string from the pattern's flags (ir-spec 10-reflags, s543m's #5402 added the charset letter there); `n` (no capture) is accepted by the compiler and honoured by the matcher but not listed among the flags the string shows.  perl's order is `^` + charset + `msixxn` letters as given ... measure perl for `qr/x/nix`, `qr/x/xxn` before writing the order.
